  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Solve each of the following equations by first changing to a common base:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\log_3 x-\\log_9(x)=3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=729$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_3 x-\\frac12\\log_3 x&=3\\\\\\frac12\\log_3 x&=3\\\\\\log_3 x&=6\\\\x&=3^6\\\\x&=729\\end{align*}$$\nThe solution satisfies $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2 x+\\log_x(2)=2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=2$",
          "worked": "Let $k=\\log_2 x$, where $x>0$, $x\\ne1$ and $k\\ne0$. Then $\\log_x2=\\frac{1}{k}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}k+\\frac1k&=2\\\\k^2+1&=2k\\\\k^2-2k+1&=0\\\\(k-1)^2&=0\\\\k&=1\\\\x&=2^1\\\\x&=2\\end{align*}$$\nThis satisfies the original equation and its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2(x-5)=\\log_4(x-2)+1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=11$\n\nChange to base $2$ and reject the root outside $x>5$.",
          "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>5$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2(x-5)&=\\frac12\\log_2(x-2)+1\\\\2\\log_2(x-5)&=\\log_2(x-2)+2\\\\\\log_2((x-5)^2)&=\\log_2(4(x-2))\\\\(x-5)^2&=4(x-2)\\\\x^2-10x+25&=4x-8\\\\x^2-14x+33&=0\\\\(x-3)(x-11)&=0\\\\x&=3\\text{ or }11\\end{align*}$$\nReject $x=3$ because $x>5$. At $x=11$, both sides equal $\\log_2 6$. Therefore $x=11$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2 x=9\\log_x2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac18\\text{ or }8$",
          "worked": "Let $k=\\log_2 x$, where $x>0$, $x\\ne1$ and $k\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}k&=\\frac9k\\\\k^2&=9\\\\k&=-3\\text{ or }3\\\\x&=2^{-3}\\text{ or }2^3\\\\x&=\\frac18\\text{ or }8\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values satisfy the original equation and its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2 x+\\log_4 x=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\sqrt[3]{4}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2 x+\\frac12\\log_2 x&=1\\\\\\frac32\\log_2 x&=1\\\\\\log_2 x&=\\frac23\\\\x&=2^{2/3}\\\\x&=\\sqrt[3]{4}\\end{align*}$$\nThe solution satisfies $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\log_4 x+4\\log_x4=4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=16$",
          "worked": "Let $k=\\log_4 x$, where $x>0$, $x\\ne1$ and $k\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}k+\\frac4k&=4\\\\k^2+4&=4k\\\\k^2-4k+4&=0\\\\(k-2)^2&=0\\\\k&=2\\\\x&=4^2\\\\x&=16\\end{align*}$$\nThis satisfies the original equation and its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$3\\log_x10+\\log_{10}x=4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=10\\text{ or }1000$",
          "worked": "Let $k=\\log_{10}x$, where $x>0$, $x\\ne1$ and $k\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac3k+k&=4\\\\3+k^2&=4k\\\\k^2-4k+3&=0\\\\(k-1)(k-3)&=0\\\\k&=1\\text{ or }3\\\\x&=10^1\\text{ or }10^3\\\\x&=10\\text{ or }1000\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values satisfy the original equation and its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q3-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$\\log_4 x-\\log_x4=4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=4^{2-\\sqrt5}\\text{ or }4^{2+\\sqrt5}$\n\nUse $k=\\log_4x$ to obtain $k^2-4k-1=0$.",
          "worked": "Let $k=\\log_4 x$, where $x>0$, $x\\ne1$ and $k\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}k-\\frac1k&=4\\\\k^2-1&=4k\\\\k^2-4k-1&=0\\\\k&=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{16+4}}2\\\\k&=2\\pm\\sqrt5\\\\x&=4^{2-\\sqrt5}\\text{ or }4^{2+\\sqrt5}\\end{align*}$$\nBoth exponents are nonzero, so both values satisfy the domain and original equation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      }
    ]
  },
